title: The NOT gate
check: behaves
parts: not
mode: draw
chapter: Signals and gates
---- intro
A **gate** is a part that looks at its inputs and decides what its output is. The simplest is the **NOT gate**: it turns a 1 into a 0, and a 0 into a 1. It always does the opposite.

| a | y |
|---|---|
| 0 | 1 |
| 1 | 0 |

This table is called a **truth table**: one row for every value the inputs can have, and what the output is then.

You make wires yourself: **drag from the small square on the right of one part** (its output) **to the small square on the left of another** (its input).
---- task
Wire the switch `a` into the NOT gate, and the NOT gate into the light `y`. Then click `a` and watch the light do the opposite.
---- start
input a 0 20
not n1 120 20
led y 260 20
---- reference
input a
led y
y = ~a
---- answer
input a 0 20
not n1 120 20
led y 260 20
a -> n1.in
n1 -> y
---- hint1
Two wires are missing. Start at the small square on the right edge of the switch `a`, hold the mouse down, and drag to the square on the left of the NOT gate.
---- hint2
Now the second wire: from the square on the right of the NOT gate (after the little circle) to the square on the left of the light `y`.
---- solution
The NOT gate goes between the switch and the light: `a` into the gate, the gate into `y`. When `a` is 0 the light is on, and when `a` is 1 it is off.
---- done
The light is on when the switch is **off**: the NOT gate inverts. Gates like this are the building blocks of everything else, and you will combine them from now on.
